	abstract = {Objectives. I test the impact of Oregon's vote-by-mail system on voter turnout. Methods. To determine the impact, I create a cross-sectional time-series regression model of state turnout in presidential elections from 1980 to 2004 and mid-term elections from 1982 to 2006. Results. I find that Oregon's turnout increases by around 10 percentage points of registered voters in both presidential and mid-term elections due to the voting-by-mail reform. Conclusions. These results suggest that one of the reasons that the United States has comparatively lower turnout is due to its more onerous voting procedures.},
